-- title:
--   Maximal Hilbert growth forces relations in degrees ≤ 1
-- statement:
--   Let $n, m, g$ be natural numbers, let $K$ be a field, and let $F_1,\dots,F_g \in K[x_0,\dots,x_n] =$ `MvPolynomial (Fin (n+1)) K` be polynomials with $F_i$ homogeneous of degree $m$ for every $i$ (in Mathlib's sense, so $F_i = 0$ is allowed). Write $J =$ `Ideal.span (Set.range F)` for the ideal they generate, and for an ideal $I$ and a degree $e$ let `piece I e` denote the $K$-vector space quotient of the space of degree-$e$ forms by the subspace of those lying in $I$, i.e. the degree-$e$ graded piece $\bigl(K[x]/I\bigr)_e$ when $I$ is homogeneous. Assume the maximal-growth hypothesis: for every ideal $J'$ of $K[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ that is the span of some set of forms of degree $m$, if $\dim_K(\mathrm{piece}\ J'\ m) = \dim_K(\mathrm{piece}\ J\ m)$ then $\dim_K(\mathrm{piece}\ J'\ (m+1)) \le \dim_K(\mathrm{piece}\ J\ (m+1))$. Let $d$ be a natural number and let $c_1,\dots,c_g$ be forms of degree $d$ with $\sum_i c_i F_i = 0$. Then the tuple $(c_i)_i$ lies in the $K[x_0,\dots,x_n]$-submodule of $K[x_0,\dots,x_n]^g$ spanned by those tuples $(l_i)_i$ for which there is a single $d' \le 1$ with every $l_i$ homogeneous of degree $d'$ and $\sum_i l_i F_i = 0$.
--
--   This is the first-syzygy consequence of Gotzmann's regularity statement for ideals generated in a single degree $m$ whose Hilbert function grows maximally from degree $m$ to degree $m+1$: all relations among the generators are generated by relations with coefficients of degree at most one. It feeds into [`AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertFunctor.exists_point_I_eq_of_projective_piece_succ`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertFunctor.exists_point_I_eq_of_projective_piece_succ), within the development of Hilbert-function bounds for homogeneous ideals; the proof cites Macaulay-type bounds on $\dim_K(\mathrm{piece}\ J\ (m+1))$ in terms of [`Nat.macaulayPow`](def/Nat_MacaulayPow.html#L7), the existence of monomial ideals attaining them, the behaviour of `piece` under base change, and a general-hyperplane statement giving $(J : \ell)_m = J_m$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPolynomial_relation_mem_span_of_forall_finrank_piece_succ_le.lean

